WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.425
Injection happens when untrusted input changes the meaning of something the application

00:00:04.425 --> 00:00:06.810
sends to an interpreter or downstream system.

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:10.765
That something might be a database query, command, document, template, script, URL,

00:00:10.765 --> 00:00:13.180
header, protocol message, or API request.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:16.140
The security failure is not simply that input was accepted.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:19.755
The failure is that data was allowed to become instructions or structure in a context

00:00:19.755 --> 00:00:21.240
where it should have remained data.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.840
JavaScript and Node.js applications encounter many interpreters.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:29.430
A backend may build SQL queries, NoSQL queries, shell commands, template output,

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:34.150
JSON documents, URLs, HTTP headers, and messages to downstream APIs.

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:39.145
A frontend may render HTML, set attributes, build URLs, update the DOM, parse JSON,

00:00:39.145 --> 00:00:41.625
and interact with browser security rules.

00:00:41.625 --> 00:00:46.060
Each context has its own rules for what characters, types, structures, and APIs mean.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:48.800
This is why injection prevention is context-specific.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.400
Input validation checks whether data has the shape the application expects.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.495
Parameterization keeps values separate from query structure.

00:00:55.495 --> 00:00:58.960
Output encoding makes data safe for a specific rendering context.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.215
Escaping may be useful only when it matches the exact interpreter.

00:01:02.215 --> 00:01:05.280
Safe API design avoids string-built instructions when possible.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.940
These controls are related, but they are not interchangeable.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:12.170
A common mistake is trying to memorize dangerous inputs instead of understanding sensitive

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:16.140
sinks. The safer mental model is to ask where untrusted data enters, where it flows,

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:17.640
and where it is interpreted.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:22.400
If the data reaches a database, shell, template engine, browser, file path, URL, header,

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:26.960
or external API, the developer must choose a control appropriate to that destination.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:30.480
The goal is simple to state and demanding to implement: keep data as data.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:34.680
JavaScript developers prevent injection by designing application boundaries so untrusted

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:36.340
input cannot change command meaning,

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:40.960
query structure, document structure, or executable logic.
